# Native console cleanup proposal — NOT EXECUTED / approval pending

Automatic approval review rejected the first proposed TerminateProcess diagnostic. Its stated reason was that PROCESS_TERMINATE can disrupt a terminal and was not explicitly authorized despite the recorded ownership checks. No TerminateProcess invocation has executed. Do not execute this inert `.data` artifact without explicit user authorization.

Actual native evidence in ../smoke1 establishes that hidden piped Windows PowerShell creates one headless conhost child. GetConsoleProcessList reports only the helper itself; GetConsoleWindow returns zero. A fixed Toolhelp32 snapshot observes the conhost ParentProcessId equal to the helper PID. Native QueryFullProcessImageName verifies the held process handle names the actual system conhost.exe. FreeConsole succeeds, but WaitForSingleObject on that exact handle remains unsignaled after 1000 ms while PowerShell is alive. The original failure and each diagnostic remain preserved; none is relabeled a pass.

The proposed helper captures redirected stdin/stdout before startup cleanup. It requires exactly one console member, self; exactly one child process, conhost.exe; a native process handle and the trusted system conhost image path. It detaches with FreeConsole. Only if the verified held conhost process is still active would it call TerminateProcess on that handle and wait at most 1000 ms. It never targets a caller PID, process name search result without ownership checks, another terminal, or an arbitrary executable. Only after confirmed termination would any request parsing, native path acquisition or valid response frame occur. Startup failure yields safe exit 22, no frame and no diagnostic. The supervisor remains responsible for actual helper/transport exit confirmation and terminal failure cleanup.

The user is asked to authorize this bounded termination of the helper's positively identified exclusively owned console host. This is a proposed implementation of the existing owned-process lifecycle requirement, not a protocol change. The reviewed script must still pass native regression and the process observer before acceptance. Production helper bytes have not been replaced by this proposal.